From: Alexis Bouzigues <BouziguesAlexis@JohnDeere.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:56:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] mm: nommu: set MMF_OOM_SKIP in exit_mmap()

On NOMMU, continued memory pressure can leave a parent livelocked in an
allocation after its child has been OOM-killed and has exited.  The child
has already freed its mappings, but until the parent collects it with
waitpid(), it remains visible to the OOM killer as an existing victim.
The OOM killer consequently defers selecting another victim, and the
allocator keeps retrying without making progress.  A parent allocating
before waiting for its child can therefore prevent the very waitpid()
needed to end the retry loop.

This was encountered while running a benchmark to measure memory
reductions and reproduced with a small /init program on v7.3-rc7 under
qemu-system-arm.  It has only been observed in benchmarks, not reported in
a deployed application.  The relevant sequence is a parent allocating
before collecting an exited child while memory pressure persists; freeing
the child's memory alone need not relieve that pressure.

Commit 212925802454 ("mm: oom: let oom_reap_task and exit_mmap run
concurrently") moved setting MMF_OOM_SKIP from the shared __mmput() into
the MMU-only exit_mmap().  Losing the flag on NOMMU was a side effect: the
problem is that an exited OOM victim is no longer hidden from the OOM
killer, not coordination with the OOM reaper.  The OOM reaper is not built
on NOMMU.  Here, collecting the child means waitpid().

Set MMF_OOM_SKIP in the NOMMU exit_mmap() after the last delete_vma(),
when the mappings have been freed.  This restores the previous NOMMU
behavior and allows the OOM killer to consider another victim instead of
waiting for an exited one.  It does not provide a general forward progress
guarantee for the inherently fragile NOMMU OOM handling.

[akpm@linux-foundation.org: chagelog updates per Michel]
